[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"User Management, Roles, and Row-Level Security (RLS) in PostgreSQL"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Secure database design requires controlling who can connect to the database, what objects they can access, and which specific rows they are permitted to view or modify. PostgreSQL provides a robust security model combining standard role-based access control (RBAC) with advanced Row-Level Security (RLS)."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Database Users and Roles"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"In modern PostgreSQL, users and groups are unified under the concept of 'roles'. A role can be thought of as a database user, a group, or both. To create a role with login privileges and a password, use the CREATE ROLE or CREATE USER statement."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"CREATE ROLE app_user WITH LOGIN PASSWORD 'SecurePassword123!';"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also create administrative roles or groups that inherit permissions:"},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"CREATE ROLE db_analyst WITH NOLOGIN;
GRANT app_user TO db_analyst;"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Granting and Revoking Privileges"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once roles are created, you must explicitly grant them permissions to interact with schemas, tables, and sequences. By default, new roles have very few privileges."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"GRANT CONNECT ON DATABASE production_db TO app_user;
GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO app_user;
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE ON TABLE orders TO app_user;"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Implementing Row-Level Security (RLS)"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"While table-level privileges restrict access to entire tables, Row-Level Security (RLS) restricts which rows a user can return or modify via standard queries. RLS must first be enabled on a specific table."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"ALTER TABLE orders ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Next, you create security policies that evaluate expressions for each row. PostgreSQL supplies the current_user or session identifier variables to help filter data dynamically."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation_policy ON orders
FOR ALL
USING (tenant_id = current_setting('app.current_tenant_id', true));"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Best Practices for RLS and Connection Pooling"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY if table owners should also be restricted by the policies."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"When using connection pools (like PgBouncer), avoid relying solely on session-level variables without resetting them. Use transaction-scoped configuration settings where applicable."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Index the columns utilized in your RLS policies (such as tenant_id or user_id) to prevent severe performance degradation during sequential scans."},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"By properly combining robust user management, principle-of-least-privilege grants, and Row-Level Security, you can build secure multi-tenant architectures directly inside PostgreSQL."}]
